this is Sansa for ian's Cemetery just  outside the town of monks in Belgium and  this will be the focus for the  commemorations to remember the centenary  of the outbreak of World War one it has  been chosen not because it's the largest  cemetery of its kind not by some way it  is one of the most beautiful however and  it has quite a poignant story attached  to it on the 23rd of August 1914 the  British Expeditionary Force were camped  in their positions  just outside Mons by one edge of the  canal the morning was misty and damp  they had just arrived in Belgium and  they didn't know the size of the German  force that they faced but by 10 o'clock  in the morning the Summer Sun had burnt  off much of the mist and it became quite  clear that the enemy was far bigger than  they first thought the British fought  valiantly but to all intents and  purposes they were grossly outnumbered  and by evening they were in retreat they  had lost the Battle of Mons the first  victory of the Great War was Germany's  the following year 1915 the Germans dug  up the bodies that had died in this area  both British and German and they wanted  to bury them here on this patch of land  and the man who owned this land said you  can have it I'll give it to you on one  condition and that condition is that you  bury the British with the same respect  as you bury your own and the Germans did  exactly that by 1919 after the end of  the war it went into the hands of what  was then known as the Imperial War  Graves Commission now the Commonwealth  War Graves Commission and they to this  day have looked after it with the same  traditions and the same love and care  and attention as they do all of their  graveyards around the world and in this  cemetery there is roughly the same  number  of Commonwealth graves as there are  German 229 Commonwealth 284 German there  are two particularly significant graves  the first that a private John Parr the  Middlesex regiment killed on the 21st of  August 1914 aged 18 he was shot dead by  a sniper's bullet from a German rifle he  was the first British soldier killed on  the Western Front in the Great War and  then there is the grave of private GE  Ellison George Ellison of the 5th  Lancers killed on the 11th of November  1918 aged 40 hours before the  Declaration of Armistice was signed  George Allison was the last British  soldier to be killed on the Western  Front in life they fought for the same  king and country but they didn't know  each other in death their graves by  sheer coincidence faced one another
 

